const faq = [
  { q: 'Where does the Chihuahuan Raven live?', a: 'The Chihuahuan Raven (Corvus cryptoleucus) is confined to the Chihuahuan Desert and adjacent arid grasslands of the southwestern USA and northern Mexico. Its US range includes south-eastern Colorado, western Kansas, Oklahoma panhandle, the Texas panhandle and Trans-Pecos Texas, New Mexico, and south-eastern Arizona. In Mexico, it is widespread in Chihuahua, Sonora, Coahuila, Zacatecas, and adjacent states. It is entirely absent from Central America and from the more mesic habitats of the eastern US.' },
  { q: 'Does the Chihuahuan Raven overlap with the Common Raven?', a: 'Yes, in a limited zone. The two species meet in the southern Great Plains (south-eastern Colorado, western Kansas, Oklahoma and Texas panhandles) and in New Mexico and Arizona, where Common Ravens occupy adjacent rocky and montane terrain. Direct overlap in the same habitat is limited because Common Ravens prefer rocky, hilly terrain while Chihuahuan Ravens use open grassland and flat desert. In winter, mixed flocks sometimes occur at food sources in the overlap zone.' },
  { q: 'Is the Chihuahuan Raven declining?', a: 'Chihuahuan Raven populations have declined in the northern parts of their range (southern Great Plains) due to changes in land use — conversion of native grassland to cultivation and changes in livestock husbandry that reduce carcass availability. Some studies have noted population contractions at the northern range edge. The species is currently classified as Least Concern by IUCN, but North American Breeding Bird Survey data suggest long-term population declines in some regions.' },
];
