    <h1>Chihuahuan Raven Range: Where This Species Lives</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Chihuahuan Raven (<em>Corvus cryptoleucus</em>) is confined to the Chihuahuan Desert and southern Great Plains — south-eastern Colorado and Kansas south through Texas, New Mexico, Arizona, and into northern and central Mexico. It occupies open, arid grassland, desert scrub, and agricultural land. It does not occur outside North America, and its US range is entirely south and west of the Missouri River.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Distribution</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Chihuahuan Raven's range is centred on the Chihuahuan Desert and adjacent arid grasslands:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>United States:</strong> south-eastern Colorado; western Kansas and the Oklahoma panhandle; the Texas panhandle, Trans-Pecos Texas, and south Texas; most of New Mexico; south-eastern Arizona. The northern range boundary roughly follows the 1,000 m elevation contour of the southern Great Plains.</li>
      <li><strong>Mexico:</strong> widespread in the arid interior — Chihuahua, Sonora, Sinaloa, Durango, Coahuila, Nuevo León, Zacatecas, San Luis Potosí, and extending south to Hidalgo. Mostly absent from tropical coastal lowlands and the dense forests of southern Mexico.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The core of the range — the Chihuahuan Desert itself, covering much of southern New Mexico, Trans-Pecos Texas, and the northern Mexican plateau — has the highest raven densities. The species is essentially endemic to the Chihuahuan biogeographic region.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Habitat Preferences</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Within its range, the Chihuahuan Raven uses open habitats:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Primary:</strong> semi-arid grassland (primarily native shortgrass and mixed-grass prairie), desert scrub with scattered shrubs, and open agricultural land — cultivated fields, pasture, and rangeland with a patchy tree or shrub structure.</li>
      <li><strong>Secondary:</strong> roadsides, landfills, and feed lots in agricultural areas, which provide concentrated food resources.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoided:</strong> dense woodland, tall-tree forest, rocky terrain and cliffs (which are favoured by the Common Raven in the same region).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The Chihuahuan Raven's near-absence from rocky and montane habitats within its geographic range is a key ecological difference from the sympatric Common Raven — they partition the landscape by terrain type.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Range Changes and Population</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> There is evidence of range contraction in the northern Great Plains portion of the range, associated with grassland conversion and changes in livestock management. Historical accounts from the 19th century document larger Great Plains populations around bison carcasses — the near-extinction of bison in the 1870s–1880s is thought to have caused a northern range contraction. Current IUCN status is Least Concern, but North American BBS data suggest long-term declines.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

